Help me create a “Share this form with support” for my website so I can view Everytime they type a letter into all text fields on full website

Yes. Build an **opt-in** support-sharing mode that sends each edit to a private support dashboard. You can install the listener across your website, including dynamically added fields, using a document-level event listener rather than attaching a handler to every field.[1][2]

I would not automatically collect every text field: that can expose passwords, payment details, recovery codes, or private messages. Instead, enable sharing site-wide but explicitly mark which fields support may see.

## 1. Add a clear consent control

Place this in your shared site layout. This version deliberately stops sharing when the page reloads or the user navigates to another full page; it does not silently remember consent.

```html
<section aria-label="Live support sharing">
  <p>
    Share selected form fields with support while you type,
    including text you have not submitted.
    Passwords and sensitive fields are not shared.
    Sharing ends when you stop it or leave this page.
  </p>

  <button id="support-start" type="button">
    Share this form with support
  </button>

  <button id="support-stop" type="button" hidden>
    Stop sharing
  </button>

  <p id="support-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">
    Not sharing
  </p>
</section>
```

Explicitly identify shareable fields with a stable, unique key:

```html
<label>
  Order description
  <textarea data-support-share="order-description"></textarea>
</label>

<label>
  Product search
  <input type="search" data-support-share="product-search">
</label>

<!-- Never mark sensitive fields as shareable. -->
<label>
  Password
  <input type="password" autocomplete="current-password">
</label>

<!-- This excludes a whole section, even if a field is marked. -->
<div data-support-private>
  <label>
    Recovery code
    <input type="text">
  </label>
</div>
```

## 2. Add the browser-side sharing code

This sends the field’s current value on each `input` event, covering typing, deletion, and paste rather than merely logging physical keystrokes. WebSockets carry those updates to your backend.[3][4]

This is frontend integration code, not a complete deployable service: the backend endpoints described below must exist.

```javascript
const startButton = document.querySelector("#support-start");
const stopButton = document.querySelector("#support-stop");
const status = document.querySelector("#support-status");

let socket = null;
let starting = false;

function setSharingUI(active, message) {
  startButton.hidden = active;
  stopButton.hidden = !active;
  status.textContent = message;
}

function stopSharing(message = "Not sharing") {
  const previous = socket;
  socket = null;

  if (previous) previous.close(1000, "Sharing ended");
  setSharingUI(false, message);
}

startButton.addEventListener("click", async () => {
  if (starting || socket) return;

  starting = true;
  startButton.disabled = true;
  status.textContent = "Connecting to support…";

  try {
    // Must validate the visitor's session and CSRF token server-side.
    const response = await fetch("/api/support/sessions", {
      method: "POST",
      credentials: "same-origin",
      headers: {
        "Content-Type": "application/json",
        "X-CSRF-Token":
          document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]')?.content ?? ""
      },
      body: JSON.stringify({ consentVersion: "live-fields-v1" })
    });

    if (!response.ok) throw new Error("Session creation failed");

    // Server returns a same-origin WebSocket path.
    const { websocketPath } = await response.json();
    const url = new URL(websocketPath, location.origin);

    if (url.origin !== location.origin) {
      throw new Error("Unexpected WebSocket destination");
    }

    url.protocol = location.protocol === "https:" ? "wss:" : "ws:";

    const connection = new WebSocket(url);
    socket = connection;

    connection.addEventListener("open", () => {
      if (socket !== connection) return;
      setSharingUI(true, "Sharing selected fields with support");
    });

    connection.addEventListener("close", () => {
      if (socket === connection) {
        socket = null;
        setSharingUI(false, "Sharing ended");
      }
    });

    connection.addEventListener("error", () => {
      if (socket === connection) {
        stopSharing("Connection failed. Nothing is being shared.");
      }
    });
  } catch {
    stopSharing("Could not start sharing.");
  } finally {
    starting = false;
    startButton.disabled = false;
  }
});

stopButton.addEventListener("click", () => stopSharing());
window.addEventListener("pagehide", () => stopSharing());

document.addEventListener("input", (event) => {
  if (!socket || socket.readyState !== WebSocket.OPEN) return;

  const field = event.target;

  if (!(field instanceof HTMLInputElement) &&
      !(field instanceof HTMLTextAreaElement)) return;

  if (!field.matches("[data-support-share]")) return;
  if (field.closest("[data-support-private]")) return;

  // An allowlist is safer than trying to detect every secret.
  if (field instanceof HTMLInputElement &&
      !["text", "search"].includes(field.type)) return;

  const autocomplete = field.autocomplete || "";
  if (/(password|one-time-code|cc-)/i.test(autocomplete)) return;

  // Stop rather than accumulate an unbounded transmission queue.
  if (socket.bufferedAmount > 64 * 1024) {
    stopSharing("Sharing stopped because the connection is too slow.");
    return;
  }

  socket.send(JSON.stringify({
    type: "field-update",
    field: field.dataset.supportShare,
    value: field.value,
    timestamp: Date.now()
  }));
}, { capture: true });
```

The delegated listener also handles eligible inputs added later to the same document. WebSockets do not automatically handle backpressure, so the example stops sharing if outgoing data begins accumulating.[1][2][5]

## 3. Build the backend and support dashboard

The backend should implement this flow:

```text
Visitor explicitly starts sharing
           ↓
POST /api/support/sessions
           ↓
Authenticated visitor WebSocket
           ↓
Server checks session ownership and message limits
           ↓
Only the assigned, authenticated support agent receives updates
           ↓
Dashboard displays each field's current value
```

Production requirements:

- Authenticate both the visitor session and support agent; a session ID alone must not grant viewing access.
- Validate the WebSocket `Origin`, session ownership, message schema, field keys, and value sizes.
- Use HTTPS/WSS in production.
- Expire sessions and revoke access on stop or disconnect.
- Do not log raw field values or retain typing history by default.
- Render incoming values using `textContent`, never `innerHTML`.
- Review every allowlisted field before release; field-type filters cannot reliably detect secrets entered into ordinary text boxes.

## Scope and limitations

This implementation shares new edits after consent—not pre-existing field values—and displays text updates rather than a visual copy of the page. It covers ordinary inputs and textareas in the current document, not third-party embedded payment forms, other tabs, or arbitrary rich-text editors.

To make it work across your full website, put the controls and script in your shared layout and mark approved fields on each page. For full-page navigation, either ask again as this example does, or deliberately design a time-limited cross-page consent session with a visible Stop control on every page.

What is your website built with—WordPress, Shopify, React/Next.js, or plain HTML—and what backend do you use?

